    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          A faucet-mount filter is the best value in home water filtration, and also the one most often returned, because it does not fit the faucet. Check your spout before anything else. If it is a fixed spout with threads at the aerator, the PUR PLUS PFM400H is the one to buy: its filter is certified by IAPMO R&amp;T to NSF/ANSI 42, 53 and 401, including lead and total PFAS. If it is a pull-down sprayer, none of these will fit, and you should skip to the section on alternatives.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="what-it-is" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. What a Faucet Filter Does, and Where It Fits Among Your Options
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How a Faucet-Mount Filter Works</h3>

          <p>
            A faucet-mount filter screws onto the end of your kitchen spout in place of the aerator and pushes tap water through a small carbon block on demand. A lever or knob on the housing switches between filtered and unfiltered water, so you can drink and cook with filtered water and still wash pans with the full, unfiltered flow. There is no plumbing, no drilling and nothing under the sink. Most install in a few minutes with the adapters in the box, and they come off just as easily when you move.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Where It Fits Among Your Options</h3>

          <p>
            That puts it in a specific slot in the lineup. A pitcher holds filtered water in the fridge but makes you wait for gravity to refill it; our <a href="/best-water-filter-pitcher/">best water filter pitcher</a> guide covers that route. An under-sink system filters more water at a better flow, with longer-lasting cartridges, but needs a cold-water connection and, often, a dedicated faucet; that is what the <a href="/best-under-sink-water-filter/">best under sink water filter</a> guide is for. A faucet filter sits between the two: filtered water on tap like an under-sink unit, with the low commitment of a pitcher.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">It Does Not Treat the Whole House</h3>

          <p>
            What it does not do is treat the whole house. Showers, laundry and the ice maker all still get untreated water. If your problem is sediment through every fixture, chloramine in the shower or a private well with iron and bacteria, read our <a href="/best-water-filtration-system-for-home/">best water filtration system for home</a> guide instead, because no faucet attachment is built for that job.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Trade-Off: Cartridge Life</h3>

          <p>
            The trade-off is cartridge life. The PUR and Brita filters on this list are rated for 100 gallons, which a household that drinks and cooks with filtered water can use up in two to four months. Cheaper per-unit cartridges make that a modest running cost, but it is a recurring one, and it is the main reason people who are happy with a faucet filter eventually move under the sink.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE SHORT VERSION</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Buy a faucet filter for drinking and cooking water, on a fixed spout, in a home where you can't or won't plumb.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">It is the cheapest way to get water certified for lead reduction straight from the tap. It is the wrong tool for pull-down faucets, whole-house problems or anyone who wants to forget about cartridges for a year.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="will-it-fit" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Will It Fit Your Faucet? Check This Before You Buy
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Pull-Out and Handheld Faucets Usually Do Not Fit</h3>

          <p>
            Fit is the most common reason faucet filters go back. PUR's own support pages say its faucet systems fit most standard faucets and that pull-out and handheld faucets are not compatible. Waterdrop's listings say the same for its faucet filters. Brita describes its system as fitting most standard faucets without tools. In practice, that rules out a large share of modern kitchen faucets, because pull-down and pull-out sprayers are now the default on most new models, as our <a href="/best-kitchen-faucet/">best kitchen faucet</a> guide shows.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why Fit Is Mechanical</h3>

          <p>
            The reason is mechanical. A faucet-mount filter needs a fixed, threaded aerator at the end of a rigid spout, because it hangs a few ounces of plastic and water off that thread. A pull-down spray head has no removable aerator in the usual sense, its outlet threads are often proprietary, and the head is designed to dock magnetically or with a counterweight. Hang a filter off it and the head will not dock, the hose weight will not retract it, and the spray modes stop working.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How to Check Your Faucet</h3>

          <p>
            To check your faucet, unscrew the aerator at the tip of the spout. If it comes off and leaves either a thread on the outside of the spout (male thread) or a thread on the inside (female thread), a faucet filter can almost certainly be made to fit with the adapters in the box. If there is no aerator to remove, or the spout is a spray head on a hose, stop here. Also check the clearance: the filter body hangs below and to the side of the spout, and on a shallow sink or a low-arc faucet it can sit uncomfortably close to the basin.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Sensor Faucets and Other Catches</h3>

          <p>
            Two other things catch people out. Faucets with a built-in sensor or touch control usually still have a threaded aerator, but the filter body can block the sensor's field of view. And some faucets marketed as fixed spouts still have a spray mode button on the head; the filter will usually fit, but you lose that button while it is on. If the included adapters do not match your threads, PUR's FAQ says to contact the company for an adapter kit before giving up.
          </p>

