        <div id="certifications" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Certifications by Standard: What Each Filter Has Actually Earned
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Only Named Certifications Carry Weight</h3>

          <p>
            Every box in this aisle claims to reduce a long list of contaminants. The claims that carry weight are the ones backed by a certification to a named NSF/ANSI standard, issued by an accredited third party such as NSF, IAPMO R&amp;T or the Water Quality Association, and published in a performance data sheet that lists the reduction for each substance. Here is what each standard means in plain terms.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What Each Standard Covers</h3>

          <p>
            NSF/ANSI 42 covers aesthetic effects: chlorine taste and odor, and fine particulates. Almost every carbon filter can pass it, and it says nothing about health. NSF/ANSI 53 covers health effects, and this is the one that matters for lead, mercury, volatile organic chemicals and, since its PFAS protocols were added, PFOA, PFOS and total PFAS. NSF/ANSI 401 covers what the standard calls incidental contaminants and emerging compounds: trace pharmaceuticals, herbicides, bisphenol A and microplastics. NSF/ANSI 372 is different again: it certifies that the materials in the device are lead-free, not that the filter removes any lead from your water.
          </p>

