          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Waterdrop: The One People Misread</h3>

          <p>
            The Waterdrop FC-01 is the one people misread. Its listing says NSF certified, and it is: to NSF/ANSI 42 for chlorine and to 372 for lead-free materials. Neither standard is a lead-reduction certification. If lead is the reason you are buying a filter, the FC-01 is the wrong filter, however long its cartridge lasts.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Culligan: The Hardest to Pin Down</h3>

          <p>
            The Culligan is the hardest to pin down. Its replacement cartridge is sold as compatible with ZeroWater's ExtremeLife faucet mount, whose data sheet lists WQA certification to NSF/ANSI 42 for chlorine and particulates and NSF/ANSI 53 for PFOA and PFOS, at 400 gallons and 0.65 gpm. Retail listings for the Culligan itself variously name WQA or IAPMO as the certifier, and one adds lead. We could not find a Culligan performance data sheet that confirms lead, so we treat it as a chlorine and PFOA/PFOS filter.
