        <div id="flow-rate" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Flow Rate: Why Filtered Water Comes Out at Half a Gallon a Minute
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Slow by Design</h3>

          <p>
            The first thing new owners notice is that filtered mode is slow. That is by design. A kitchen faucet typically runs at 1.5 to 2.2 gallons per minute. Every certification in the table above was earned at a rated service flow, and for these filters that flow is about half a gallon a minute: 0.52 gpm for the PUR cartridge, 0.58 gpm for Brita, 0.5 gpm for the Waterdrop FC-01, and 0.65 gpm on the ZeroWater sheet that matches the Culligan cartridge.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Carbon Needs Contact Time</h3>

          <p>
            The rated flow matters because carbon needs contact time. Lead and PFAS reduction depends on water spending long enough in the carbon block to be adsorbed or captured, and a faster flow means less time. The certified result is valid at the rated flow and below. Most faucet filters regulate this themselves through the size of the orifice and the density of the block, which is why filtered mode feels the same however far you open the tap.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What Half a Gallon a Minute Feels Like</h3>

          <p>
            In real terms, 0.5 gpm fills an 8-ounce glass in about four seconds, a liter bottle in around 30 seconds and a large pasta pot in two to three minutes. For drinking and cooking that is fine. For filling a stockpot or a coffee urn every morning it gets tedious, and that is the point where an under-sink system with a dedicated faucet earns its extra cost.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Flow Falls as the Cartridge Loads Up</h3>

          <p>
            Flow also falls as a cartridge loads up. Sediment and rust from old pipes clog the outer surface of the block, and the stream slows before the rated capacity is reached. Brita's manual says to replace the filter early if you notice a drop in flow. If flow falls off within weeks, it is worth putting a sediment pre-filter on the supply or testing your water for iron and turbidity, because you are paying for carbon that is being blinded by grit.
          </p>

