            6. Cost per Gallon: The Number That Decides It
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Compare Cost per Gallon</h3>

          <p>
            Faucet filter bodies are cheap. The cartridges are where the money goes, so compare cost per gallon, not sticker price. The arithmetic is simple: cartridge price divided by rated gallons. A cartridge that costs $15 and is rated for 100 gallons works out at 15 cents a gallon. The same $15 spent on a 320-gallon cartridge is under 5 cents a gallon, and on a 400-gallon cartridge it is under 4 cents.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Capacity vs. Certification</h3>

          <p>
            That looks like a clear win for the long-life cartridges, and on cost alone it is. But capacity and certification pull in opposite directions here. The two 100-gallon cartridges are the two with published lead certifications. The 320-gallon Waterdrop cartridge is certified for chlorine taste and odor only. The 400-gallon Culligan has a PFOA/PFOS claim but no lead claim we could confirm. You are not comparing like with like, and the cheapest gallon is only cheap if it removes what you need removed.
          </p>

