        <div id="lead-pfas" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Lead, PFAS and Microplastics: Which Filter for Which Worry
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Start With Your Water Report</h3>

          <p>
            Start with what is actually in your water. Your utility's annual Consumer Confidence Report lists regulated contaminants at the treatment plant, and its lead service line inventory tells you whether your street still has lead pipes. Neither tells you what comes out of your own tap, which is where lead from old solder and brass fittings shows up. A home test is cheap; our guide to the <a href="/best-water-test-kit/">best water test kit</a> covers which ones measure lead properly and which are only good for hardness and chlorine.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">If Lead Is the Concern</h3>

          <p>
            If lead is the concern, buy a filter with an NSF/ANSI 53 lead certification, full stop. On this list that means the PUR PLUS, at 99.7% and 99.6% on its data sheet, or the Brita Elite faucet system, which Brita rates at 99%. The Waterdrop FC-01 has no lead-reduction certification, and we could not confirm one for the Culligan. Run the tap for a few seconds after the water has sat overnight before you drink, whatever filter you use, because stagnant water picks up the most lead.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">If PFAS Is the Concern</h3>

          <p>
            If PFAS is the concern, the PUR PLUS cartridge is the strongest documented choice in this category: its data sheet lists total PFAS reduction of 99.92% under NSF/ANSI 53. The Culligan's compatible ZeroWater data sheet lists PFOA and PFOS reduction of more than 98.9%, which covers the two most studied compounds but not the wider family. We found no PFAS claim for the Brita faucet cartridge or the Waterdrop FC-01.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">If Microplastics Are the Concern</h3>

          <p>
            Microplastics are covered by NSF/ANSI 401, using a particle-count test in the 0.5 to 1 micron range. The PUR data sheet lists 99.65%. Brita markets 99% microplastics reduction for the faucet system. The other two make microplastics claims in their listings without a published 401 certification we could find.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">None of Them Make Unsafe Water Safe</h3>

          <p>
            What none of these do is make unsafe water safe. PUR's data sheet is explicit that the filter does not filter microbes and must not be used with water that is microbiologically unsafe. Faucet filters also do nothing for hardness, so they will not stop scale; that is a job for a softener, covered in our <a href="/best-water-softener-system/">best water softener system</a> guide.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">NSF/ANSI 372 is not a lead-reduction certification.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">It means the filter's own materials are lead-free. For lead reduction, look for NSF/ANSI 53 and a lead percentage on the performance data sheet.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="care" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Hot Water, Freezing and Other Ways to Ruin a Cartridge
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Cold Water Only</h3>

          <p>
            Faucet filters are cold-water devices. PUR's data sheet gives a maximum usable water temperature of 82°F, and the others give similar limits. Hot water passing through a carbon block can release what it has captured and can warp the housing. The switch on the filter is what protects you: set it to unfiltered before you run hot water for dishes, and make it a habit, because the mixing valve on a single-handle faucet makes it easy to send warm water through without thinking.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Stay Within the Pressure Range</h3>

          <p>
            Pressure matters too. The PUR and Brita sheets list a working range of 20 to 100 psi. Most homes sit between 40 and 80 psi, but if yours runs above 100, a faucet filter will leak at the adapter or diverter, and you should fix the pressure at the regulator rather than blame the filter. Very low pressure, common in older homes with galvanized pipe, makes filtered flow painfully slow.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Flush a New Cartridge</h3>

          <p>
            A new cartridge needs flushing. Carbon fines, the black dust from the manufacturing process, come out in the first few minutes of use. Follow the maker's flush time before drinking, usually a few minutes of cold water. If the filter will sit unused for a while, for example over a long holiday, flush it again before drinking from it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Fixing Leaks at the Adapter</h3>

          <p>
            Leaks are almost always at the adapter. If the filter drips where it meets the spout, check that the right adapter is in place, that the rubber washer is seated and not doubled up, and that you have not cross-threaded it. Overtightening cracks the plastic collar. Hand-tight plus a small turn is enough on most faucets.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Drain It Before a Freeze</h3>

          <p>
            If your home freezes, for example a cabin or a seasonal property, take the filter off and drain it before winter. Water left in a carbon block that freezes can crack the cartridge and housing. The same goes for outdoor kitchens. Our guide to <a href="/how-to-winterize-outdoor-faucets/">winterizing outdoor faucets</a> covers the rest of that job.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="when-to-upgrade" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. When a Faucet Filter Is the Wrong Answer
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Where It Is the Right Answer</h3>

          <p>
            A faucet filter is the right answer for a renter, for a fixed-spout kitchen and for anyone whose concern is lead, chlorine taste or PFAS in drinking water at a modest volume. There are four situations where something else is better.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Pull-Down Sprayers</h3>

          <p>
            First, if your faucet is a pull-down sprayer, do not fight it. Either fit an under-sink filter that feeds the existing faucet or a dedicated drinking-water tap, or use a countertop unit that needs no connection. Our <a href="/best-countertop-water-filter/">countertop water filter</a> guide covers gravity-fed and faucet-diverter units side by side.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">High Daily Volume</h3>

          <p>
            Second, if you go through more than a couple of gallons of filtered water a day, the 100-gallon cartridges become a chore and a running cost. An under-sink carbon system with a 500- to 800-gallon cartridge, or one rated by the year, does the same job with fewer changes and better flow.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Dissolved Solids, Fluoride, Nitrate or Arsenic</h3>

          <p>
            Third, if your water report shows dissolved solids, fluoride, nitrate or arsenic, carbon is the wrong technology. You need reverse osmosis. If you can't plumb one in, the countertop plug-in units in our <a href="/best-reverse-osmosis-water-filter-pitcher/">reverse osmosis water filter pitcher</a> guide are the no-install route.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Whole-House Problems</h3>

          <p>
            Fourth, if the problem is in every tap, such as sediment, rotten-egg smells or well water, it is a whole-house problem. Our page on <a href="/why-does-my-water-smell-like-rotten-eggs/">why water smells like rotten eggs</a> explains how to tell a water-heater problem from a well problem, and the whole-house guide covers what to install.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do faucet water filters actually work?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The certified ones do what their data sheet says, at the rated flow and capacity. The PUR PLUS cartridge, for example, is certified by IAPMO R&amp;T to NSF/ANSI 42, 53 and 401, with lead reduction of 99.6 to 99.7% and total PFAS of 99.92% on its data sheet. A filter with only NSF/ANSI 42 is certified for chlorine taste and odor, not health contaminants.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will a faucet filter fit a pull-down faucet?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. PUR says pull-out and handheld faucets are not compatible with its faucet systems, and Waterdrop's listings exclude pull-out, handheld and spray-style faucets. A pull-down head has no standard removable aerator and needs to dock. Use an under-sink filter or a countertop unit that does not attach to the faucet instead.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is PUR or Brita better for a faucet filter?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                On published certification, PUR. Its current data sheet covers NSF/ANSI 42, 53 and 401, including total PFAS and microplastics, certified by IAPMO R&amp;T. Brita's faucet system is tested to NSF/ANSI 42 and 53 and rated for 99% lead reduction, but we found no PFAS claim for it. Both cartridges are rated for 100 gallons.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How often should you change a faucet water filter?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                At the rated gallons or the time limit, whichever comes first. That is 100 gallons or about three months for PUR and 100 gallons or about four months for Brita. The Waterdrop FC-01 is rated for 320 gallons or about three months, and the Culligan for 400 gallons. Change it sooner if flow drops noticeably.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does a faucet filter remove fluoride?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                None of the filters in this guide claims a certified fluoride reduction. Carbon is not an effective fluoride treatment. Reverse osmosis is the reliable household method, either under the sink or as a plug-in countertop unit, and activated alumina is the other option. Check your utility's water report for the fluoride level before deciding it is worth treating.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why is filtered water so slow from my faucet filter?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Because the rated flow is about half a gallon a minute: 0.52 gpm for PUR and 0.58 gpm for Brita. Carbon needs contact time to reduce lead and PFAS, and the certified results only apply at that flow. If it is much slower than when the cartridge was new, the block is clogging with sediment and is due for a change.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use a faucet filter with well water?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only if the well water has been tested and is microbiologically safe. PUR's data sheet says its filter does not filter microbes and must not be used on unsafe water or water of unknown quality. Wells with iron, sediment or sulfur will also clog a small carbon cartridge quickly, so treat those problems at the point of entry first.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying before checking the faucet</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Unscrew the aerator first. If there is no removable aerator, or the spout is a pull-down spray head, a faucet-mount filter will not fit, and PUR and Waterdrop say so in their own documentation. Returns for fit are the most common complaint in this category, and they are completely avoidable with a 30-second check.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Reading NSF/ANSI 372 as lead removal</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A filter certified only to NSF/ANSI 42 and 372 is certified for chlorine taste and lead-free materials. It is not certified to reduce lead in your water. If lead is your concern, look for NSF/ANSI 53 and a published lead reduction figure, which on this list means the PUR PLUS or the Brita Elite.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running hot water through the filter</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The PUR data sheet sets a maximum of 82°F. Hot water through a carbon block can release captured contaminants and damage the housing. Flip the diverter to unfiltered before washing up with hot water, every time, and do not use filtered mode to fill a kettle from a mixer tap set warm.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Changing cartridges by gallons alone</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Rated gallons and the calendar limit both apply, whichever comes first. A cartridge used lightly for six months has not reached 100 gallons, but it has sat wet long enough to be due. Use the indicator if the filter has one, or write the install date on the housing.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting a cheap replacement's borrowed claims</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Third-party cartridges for PUR and Brita housings are everywhere and much cheaper. A compatible cartridge does not inherit the original's certification. Check that the replacement has its own listed NSF/ANSI 53 certification before relying on it for lead or PFAS.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

